      "title": "‘Let me talk to Snez’: court hears recording of Sam Wood saying wife Snezana could recant domestic violence allegations",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/17/sam-wood-snezana-bail-domestic-violence-allegations-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Fitness influencer’s lawyer calls comment during taped conversation between client and his father ‘ill-judged’ but denies it amounts to a suggestion of interfering with a witness</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>A Queensland court has been played a prison recording of the former <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/30/sam-wood-bail-bid-court-wife-szezana-ntwnfb\">Bachelor star Sam Wood</a> sobbing as he tells his father that he needs to talk to his wife, Snezana, and that his domestic violence charges would be dropped if she recanted her statements to police.</p><p>The court of appeal is hearing a third application by Wood, 46, for bail over allegations he strangled and assaulted his wife while the family was holidaying on the Sunshine Coast.\n <br />\n <br />\n The court was played a recording of a phone conversation between Wood and his father, Andrew, immediately after he was denied bail by the supreme court this month.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/17/sam-wood-snezana-bail-domestic-violence-allegations-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-17T03:27:05"
    },
    {
      "title": "David Jones’ total debt is over $400m including millions owed to staff, administrators confirm",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/17/david-jones-creditors-meeting-debt-administrators",
      "description": "<p>Creditors meeting hears upmarket retailer owes $11m to employees and recorded a net loss of $228m last financial year</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Upmarket retailer David Jones collapsed into administration earlier this month owing well over $400m, after accelerating financial losses and reputational damage threatened its ability to stock shelves for Christmas.</p><p>About 500 creditors attended its first meeting on Tuesday, where administrators McGrathNicol broke down the 188-year-old chain’s massive debt pile, including an estimated $180m owed to suppliers and $11m to employees.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/17/david-jones-creditors-meeting-debt-administrators\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-17T02:11:43"
